            <ArticleTitle>Intestinal methane production is associated with decreased weight loss following bariatric surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>728-733</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1871-403X(16)30042-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.orcp.2016.06.006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>To determine whether methane and hydrogen on breath test affects weight loss after bariatric surgery, 156 subjects (pre-surgery BMI ≥33) were recruited ≥4 months after surgery. Pre- and post-surgery weights and BMIs were recorded. Post-surgery methane and hydrogen levels were determined. % total weight loss and % change in BMI were prorated to six months after surgery. M+/H+ subjects (N=13) exhibited lower prorated % change in BMI vs. all other subjects (N=144) (p=0.13), and significantly lower prorated % total weight loss (p=0.036). These findings may suggest that subjects with positive breath methane and hydrogen lose less weight following bariatric surgery.</AbstractText>
